The limit does only apply to artifact wishing, and it is only nominally
a limit given that the first two are free.  You can get unlucky and
have, say, Sting generated on the floor, but overall if you're cautious
and don't play around with altars, you can burn your first two wishes
out of the Castle wand for a MRtifact and a toy of your choice.

> I'm a little surprised that Derek hasn't changed the mechanics of
> artifact wishing (at least IIRC the last time I looked I didn't
> anything to that effect).

I haven't directly changed it because I'm trying to approach it from a
better angle; the other response to this illustrates part of what I'm
doing.

> Wouldn't a stricter restriction of artifact wishing or a prohibition
> of wishing for quest artifacts of other classes be something that
> would fit SporkHacks design concept?

Rather than prohibit wishing for other QAs entirely, I prefer to follow
the PYEC's lead and simply make wishing for them less attractive.  The
Mirror, for example, now provides reflection when carried for Knights,
but only when wielded for others.  The Eye became an amulet of power,
rather than an amulet of ESP, and the amulet of power itself grants
different scales of bonus for wizards compared to non-wizards.

This could happen for other artifacts as well at some point (boosting
the power of the Fart of Ahriman would certainly be tied only to the
Barbarian class, for example).

